WEBVTT

00:00:01.696 --> 00:00:03.447
Oxford Bookworms.

00:00:03.507 --> 00:00:05.408
Stage 1.

00:00:05.468 --> 00:00:07.279
Scotland.

00:00:07.339 --> 00:00:09.840
By Steve Flinders.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.402
Read by Joan Walker.

00:00:12.462 --> 00:00:24.872
Published and Copyright Oxford University Press 2010.

00:00:24.932 --> 00:00:38.722
Chapter 1 A Special Country There is nowhere like Scotland.

00:00:38.782 --> 00:00:51.806
Scotland is a country in a country.

00:00:51.866 --> 00:01:17.132
It is part of Great Britain, England, Scotland and Wales, and of the United Kingdom, England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland.

00:01:17.192 --> 00:01:27.582
Scotland is in the far northwest of Europe, between the Atlantic Ocean and the North Sea.

00:01:27.642 --> 00:01:36.190
It is often cold and grey and it rains a lot in some parts of the country.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:46.268
But the people of Scotland love their country and many visitors to Scotland love it too.

00:01:46.328 --> 00:02:09.341
They love the beautiful hills and mountains of the north, the sea and the 800 islands and the six cities, Edinburgh, Glasgow, Aberdeen, Dundee, Inverness and Sterling.

00:02:09.401 --> 00:02:12.802
The country is special.

00:02:12.862 --> 00:02:19.958
and Scottish people are special too, often warm and friendly.

00:02:20.018 --> 00:02:25.372
There are about 5 million people in Scotland.

00:02:25.432 --> 00:02:35.250
Most Scots live in the south, in or near the big cities of Edinburgh and Glasgow.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:43.336
Most of the north of the country is very empty, not many people live there.

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:54.524
A Scottish person is also called a Scott, but you cannot talk about a Scotch person.

00:02:54.584 --> 00:03:00.980
Scotch means whiskey, a drink made in Scotland.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:13.438
Scottish people are British because Scotland is part of Great Britain, but you must not call Scottish people English.

00:03:13.498 --> 00:03:18.760
The Scots and the English are different.

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:34.346
These days everyone in Scotland speaks English, but at one time people in the north and west of Scotland did not speak English.

00:03:34.406 --> 00:03:41.670
They had a different language, a beautiful language called Gaelic.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:51.269
About 60,000 people, 1% of the people in Scotland speak Gaelic now.

00:03:51.329 --> 00:04:01.326
But many more want Gaelic in their lives because it is part of the story of Scotland.

00:04:01.385 --> 00:04:06.700
Scotland is not a very hot country.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:12.475
In the summer the days are long and it can be warm.

00:04:12.535 --> 00:04:22.572
but in the winter the days can be just seven hours long and it often rains.

00:04:22.631 --> 00:04:34.257
For many years Scotland was a poor country but now things are better for most people.

00:04:34.317 --> 00:04:41.653
There is oil and gas in the sea between Scotland and Norway.

00:04:41.713 --> 00:04:53.541
Edinburgh is an important place for money and there are big banks there like the Royal Bank of Scotland.

00:04:53.601 --> 00:04:59.201
People in many countries drink Scotch whiskey.

00:04:59.261 --> 00:05:05.309
and the whiskey business makes a lot of money for Scotland.

00:05:05.369 --> 00:05:13.626
Tourists visit this beautiful country and that brings money to Scotland too.

00:05:13.686 --> 00:05:28.175
Many people love living and working there and more than 20 million visitors go to Scotland each year.

00:05:28.235 --> 00:05:46.413
Chapter 2 Scotland's Past Scotland is the oldest country in the world.

00:05:46.473 --> 00:05:54.317
Why?

00:05:54.377 --> 00:06:07.300
Because the hills of the northwest and the Hebridean Islands are more than 2,700 million years old.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:14.273
You can walk on some of the oldest rocks in the world there.

00:06:14.332 --> 00:06:20.270
People first lived there 9,000 years ago.

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:35.870
At Scarabray, on the Orkney Islands, in the far north of Scotland, you can see the houses of early people from about 5,000 years ago.

00:06:35.929 --> 00:06:45.630
The houses at Knapp of Howa, also on the Orkneys, are the oldest in Europe.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:53.215
The Romans went to Scotland, but they did not stay there for long.

00:06:53.275 --> 00:06:56.227
Between AD 122 and 128, they built Hadrian's Wall.

00:06:56.247 --> 00:07:09.129
It was 117 kilometres long and went from sea to sea across the most northern part of England.

00:07:09.188 --> 00:07:30.151
The Romans stayed in England for nearly 300 years until about AD 400 and then they left and went back to Rome.

00:07:30.211 --> 00:07:40.384
Today you can visit Hadrian's Wall in the north of England and walk along parts of it.

00:07:40.444 --> 00:07:45.214
Who were the first Scots?

00:07:45.274 --> 00:07:53.350
The people north of Hadrian's Wall were called Picts by the Romans.

00:07:53.410 --> 00:08:00.544
We can still see some of their story in their pictures in stone.

00:08:00.604 --> 00:08:05.889
But there were also Scotty from Ireland.

00:08:05.949 --> 00:08:11.372
The name Scotland comes from the Scotty.

00:08:11.432 --> 00:08:18.909
Vikings from Norway and some English people from the south.

00:08:18.969 --> 00:08:26.761
These different peoples came under one king in the eight hundreds.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:35.572
The first king of all the Scots, many people say, was Kenneth MacAlpin.

00:08:35.632 --> 00:08:41.005
He was king from 843 to 858.

00:08:41.065 --> 00:08:56.454
But the most famous Scottish king of this early time is Macbeth, 1040-1057.

00:08:56.514 --> 00:09:03.888
He is famous because Shakespeare wrote about him.

00:09:03.949 --> 00:09:18.454
For Shakespeare Macbeth was a very bad man, but he was not worse than many other kings of those early days.

00:09:18.514 --> 00:09:24.798
There were many battles between England and Scotland.

00:09:24.858 --> 00:09:33.402
One important Scott was William Wallace, about 1270 to 1305.

00:09:33.462 --> 00:09:40.743
you can learn about him in the film Braveheart.

00:09:40.802 --> 00:09:51.886
Then, in 1314, the Scottish King Robert the Bruce took his men to the Battle of Banachban.

00:09:51.946 --> 00:10:05.716
After the battle, 10,000 Englishmen were dead, and Robert became one of the most important kings in the story of Scotland.

00:10:05.776 --> 00:10:16.487
Soon after, Scotland was free and stayed free for nearly 300 years.

00:10:16.547 --> 00:10:25.502
In 1542, a little girl called Mary became Queen of Scotland.

00:10:25.562 --> 00:10:34.375
She was six days old and only the second woman to be Queen of this country.

00:10:34.435 --> 00:10:46.908
Mary, Queen of Scots, became a tall and beautiful woman, but some Scots did not want her to be Queen.

00:10:46.968 --> 00:10:58.482
Mary went to England and asked Elizabeth the English Queen for help, but she did not get it.

00:10:58.542 --> 00:11:07.324
She never returned to Scotland and died in England after 19 years.

00:11:07.384 --> 00:11:18.090
You can read Mary's story in Mary Queen of Scots, Oxford Bookworms, Stage 1.

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:29.928
Mary's son, James Stewart, became King of Scotland, and then King James I of England too.

00:11:29.988 --> 00:11:38.018
In 1707, the two countries became Great Britain.

00:11:38.078 --> 00:11:46.302
In the 1700s, Scotland was more like two countries than one.

00:11:46.362 --> 00:12:00.120
There were rich cities in the south, but there were poor country people in the highlands, the hill country in the centre and the north of Scotland.

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:11.450
At that time, Edinburgh was one of the most important cities in Europe, and many famous thinkers lived there.

00:12:11.510 --> 00:12:17.956
Then, in the 1800s, Glasgow became rich.

00:12:18.016 --> 00:12:24.771
People built big ships there, and later, trains.

00:12:24.831 --> 00:12:38.459
So, the south of Scotland had busy cities with beautiful buildings, lots of work and money.

00:12:38.519 --> 00:12:45.054
In the Highlands, things were very different.

00:12:45.114 --> 00:12:55.810
After 1714, Great Britain had German kings from Hanover in North Germany.

00:12:55.870 --> 00:13:10.356
Many Scots in the Highlands wanted a Scottish king, someone from the Stuart family, like Mary and James, not a German king in London.

00:13:10.416 --> 00:13:25.714
They wanted Charles Stuart, Bonnie Prince Charlie, the grandson of the last Stuart King, James II of England and the 7th of Scotland.

00:13:25.774 --> 00:13:32.676
Charles Stuart left France and came to Scotland.

00:13:32.736 --> 00:13:38.312
He wanted to be King of Scotland and England too.

00:13:38.372 --> 00:13:48.550
But Charles and his men lost the Battle of Colaudin near Inverness in 1746.

00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:55.012
Colaudin was the last big battle in Great Britain.

00:13:55.072 --> 00:14:05.570
After the battle, the British soldiers looked for Charles, but he went into the hills.

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:18.509
The people of the Highlands and the Islands helped him to go back to France, but life became difficult for them after that.

00:14:18.569 --> 00:14:30.480
The British soldiers stayed in the Highlands and took away houses and land from the Friends of Charles Stewart.

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:51.632
After this, many poor families left the Highlands and went to the cities in the south of Scotland or to other countries, the USA, Canada, Australia, and New Zealand.

00:14:51.692 --> 00:15:08.530
Some went because they wanted to begin a new life, but others went because the rich owners of the land in Scotland wanted to put animals there.

00:15:08.590 --> 00:15:22.024
Between 1840 and 1880, 40,000 people left just one island the island of sky.

00:15:22.084 --> 00:15:37.196
Life became more difficult in the 1900s, but oil and gas in the North Sea began to bring money to Scotland again in the 1970s.

00:15:37.256 --> 00:15:51.713
Let's look now at some of the famous places in the Scotland of today.

00:15:51.773 --> 00:16:12.052
Chapter 3 Edinburgh Many people begin a visit to Scotland in Edinburgh, the capital city.

00:16:12.112 --> 00:16:25.440
Edinburgh is an old city with many important and interesting buildings and about 470,000 people live there.

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:35.114
After London, Edinburgh is the second city for visitors in Britain.

00:16:35.174 --> 00:16:43.606
Come to Edinburgh by train from the south and you arrive at Waverly station.

00:16:43.666 --> 00:16:53.516
When you come out of the station, Edinburgh Castle is in front of you, high up on a hill.

00:16:53.576 --> 00:17:00.056
From the castle, you can see all over the city.

00:17:00.116 --> 00:17:11.831
You can see the famous one o'clock gum, and at one o'clock from Monday to Saturday, you can hear it too.

00:17:11.891 --> 00:17:15.784
It makes a very big noise.

00:17:15.844 --> 00:17:24.431
Edinburgh is built on hills, but you can walk around the city easily.

00:17:24.491 --> 00:17:32.550
From the castle, you can go down the royal mile to Holy Roode House.

00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:43.418
This building, 300 years old, is the home of Queen Elizabeth II when she comes to Edinburgh.

00:17:43.478 --> 00:17:50.108
This part of the city is called the Old Town.

00:17:50.168 --> 00:17:58.305
take a walk along Princess Street in the new town.

00:17:58.365 --> 00:18:11.780
The new town, 1767 to 1840, is more than 150 years old now, but still has this name.

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:17.716
Some shops have the famous Scottish tartans.

00:18:17.776 --> 00:18:33.673
Each clan, a big old Scottish family is called the clan, has its own tartan and in the windows you can see the different tartans for famous Scottish clans.

00:18:33.734 --> 00:18:42.126
People with a Scottish family name can buy and wear the family tartan.

00:18:42.186 --> 00:18:48.542
People without a Scottish name can buy and wear a tartan too.

00:18:48.602 --> 00:18:53.769
Edinburgh has many wonderful things.

00:18:53.829 --> 00:19:01.852
The buildings in the new town in Charlotte Square, for example, are very beautiful.

00:19:01.913 --> 00:19:05.494
There are very good museums.

00:19:05.554 --> 00:19:18.375
The National Museum of Scotland is near the Royal Mile and tells you a lot about the Scotland of yesterday and today.

00:19:18.435 --> 00:19:32.155
The National Gallery of Scotland, near the castle, has beautiful pictures from Scotland and from many other countries too.

00:19:32.215 --> 00:19:55.490
In August, thousands of people come to the Edinburgh Festival, singing, dancing, cinema, books, pictures, theatre, you can see and do hundreds of different things at the festival.

00:19:55.550 --> 00:20:09.206
And also in August, every evening for three weeks, you can go to the Edinburgh military tattoo at Edinburgh Castle.

00:20:09.266 --> 00:20:19.423
There, You can see soldiers and hear music from Scotland and from lots of other countries.

00:20:19.484 --> 00:20:31.487
On the 31st of December, everyone wants to be in the city centre for the famous Street Party for Hogmanay.

00:20:31.547 --> 00:20:36.560
That is the Scottish name for New Year's Eve.

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:44.746
But there are only 100,000 tickets and they go very quickly.

00:20:44.807 --> 00:20:51.449
The party begins in one year and finishes in the next.

00:20:51.509 --> 00:21:01.448
That is a good time.

00:21:01.509 --> 00:21:26.598
Chapter 4 Glasgow Glasgow is Scotland's biggest city and the third biggest in the United Kingdom after London and Birmingham.

00:21:26.658 --> 00:21:32.024
About 630,000 people live in the city.

00:21:32.085 --> 00:21:40.494
and about 1.2 million in and near it.

00:21:40.554 --> 00:21:52.144
It is not very far from Edinburgh, about 50 minutes by train, but it is very different.

00:21:52.204 --> 00:22:04.130
The river Clyde runs through the centre of Glasgow and it has an important part in the story of Glasgow.

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:11.222
200 years ago, Glasgow was a small town.

00:22:11.282 --> 00:22:17.606
Then British ships began to go all over the world.

00:22:17.666 --> 00:22:22.910
Big ships came up and down the river Clyde.

00:22:22.970 --> 00:22:27.162
They carried things from other countries.

00:22:27.222 --> 00:22:34.536
In the 1800s, Britain was the richest country in the world.

00:22:34.596 --> 00:22:44.789
Shipbuilding became very important and Glasgow became a city of shipbuilders.

00:22:44.849 --> 00:22:56.580
At one time it was the fourth largest city in Europe after London, Paris and Berlin.

00:22:56.640 --> 00:23:05.132
You can see some of the beautiful buildings from that time in George Square.

00:23:05.192 --> 00:23:09.554
Today, there is not much shipbuilding.

00:23:09.614 --> 00:23:17.176
Some parts of Glasgow are very poor, and many people have no work.

00:23:17.236 --> 00:23:21.968
But things are changing in Glasgow.

00:23:22.029 --> 00:23:27.090
Ask a Glaswegian, a person from Glasgow.

00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:33.359
To them, Glasgow is the friendliest city in Britain.

00:23:33.419 --> 00:23:39.016
and one of the most exciting cities in Britain too.

00:23:39.076 --> 00:23:44.578
There are lots of things to do at night in Glasgow.

00:23:44.638 --> 00:23:52.470
It is perhaps the best city in Britain after London for shopping too.

00:23:52.530 --> 00:23:56.951
Like Edinburgh, it is a green city.

00:23:57.011 --> 00:23:59.747
It has 70 parks.

00:23:59.807 --> 00:24:06.794
and you can often see the hills from the centre of the city.

00:24:06.855 --> 00:24:11.316
It is a city of museums.

00:24:11.376 --> 00:24:26.316
You can see many beautiful pictures in the Glasgow City Museum and Art Gallery and in the Borough Collection and Pollock House south of the Clyde.

00:24:26.376 --> 00:24:31.742
It is the city of Charles Renee McIntosh.

00:24:31.802 --> 00:24:41.435
About a hundred years ago, McIntosh and three friends began a new look in building.

00:24:41.495 --> 00:24:43.756
Art Nouveau.

00:24:43.816 --> 00:24:49.008
Much of McIntosh's best work is in Glasgow.

00:24:49.068 --> 00:24:54.800
His Glasgow School of Art is on Renfrew Street.

00:24:54.860 --> 00:25:06.342
After you visit it, You can have a coffee at the Willow Tea Rooms, also by Macintosh, on Socky Hall Street.

00:25:06.402 --> 00:25:12.584
And Glasgow is, of course, a big football city.

00:25:12.644 --> 00:25:28.230
You can watch Glasgow Celtic at Celtic Park, or Glasgow Rangers at the Eyebrocks Stadium on Saturdays between August and May.

00:25:28.290 --> 00:25:49.496
Chapter 5 Four Scottish Cities Aberdeen, with 192,000 people, is the third biggest city in Scotland.

00:25:49.556 --> 00:26:11.748
It is in the east of the country, on the North Sea, and it is between two rivers, the Dee and the Don.

00:26:11.808 --> 00:26:25.657
Fishing and shipbuilding were once important here, but now it is famous as the oil capital of the UK.

00:26:25.717 --> 00:26:36.504
boats and plains leave Aberdeen every day for the oil and gas fields of the North Sea.

00:26:36.564 --> 00:26:44.631
It has two universities and many wonderful parks and gardens.

00:26:44.691 --> 00:26:53.100
The musicians, Annie Lennox and Evelyn Glenney, both come from Aberdeen.

00:26:53.160 --> 00:27:04.596
Visitors to Aberdeen often go to its beautiful long beach or go climbing south of the city.

00:27:04.656 --> 00:27:13.429
Aberdeen is close to the cold and beautiful mountains called the Cairngorms.

00:27:13.489 --> 00:27:21.042
and there are more than 350 castles in this part of the country.

00:27:21.102 --> 00:27:27.223
One of them is Balmoral, the Queen's Castle.

00:27:27.283 --> 00:27:32.594
Queen Elizabeth comes here every summer.

00:27:32.654 --> 00:27:39.592
Dundee, on the river Tay, is near the North Sea too.

00:27:39.652 --> 00:27:46.688
Like Glasgow and Aberdeen, it was once important for shipbuilding.

00:27:46.748 --> 00:27:56.476
The ship Discovery was built here and left Dundee in 1901 for Antarctica.

00:27:56.536 --> 00:28:07.126
Now, more than 100 years later, the ship is back in Dundee again and you can visit it there.

00:28:07.186 --> 00:28:13.534
Dundee is also famous for marmalade.

00:28:13.594 --> 00:28:26.044
The Keeler family began making orange marmalade here in 1797 and it became famous all over the world.

00:28:26.104 --> 00:28:34.740
Inverness is the only city in the Highlands.

00:28:34.800 --> 00:28:42.404
The best bagpipers in the world come here to play every September.

00:28:42.464 --> 00:28:48.247
It is close to the beautiful lake called Loch Ness.

00:28:48.307 --> 00:28:52.562
Loch is the Gaelic word for lake.

00:28:52.622 --> 00:29:03.734
A very big animal called the Loch Ness Monster or Nessie lives in Loch Ness.

00:29:03.794 --> 00:29:13.360
An old story says this, and thousands of tourists go there every year to look for it.

00:29:13.420 --> 00:29:16.683
But Nessie does not come.

00:29:16.743 --> 00:29:21.612
Perhaps this is just a story for tourists.

00:29:21.672 --> 00:29:29.172
Then there is Stirling to the northeast of Glasgow.

00:29:29.232 --> 00:29:36.094
Many visitors go through Stirling when they go to the Highlands.

00:29:36.154 --> 00:29:45.750
It has a wonderful castle, one of the biggest and most important castles in Scotland.

00:29:45.810 --> 00:29:51.266
The Battle of Banacburn in 1314 was near here.

00:29:51.327 --> 00:30:09.074
And here Mary became Queen of Scotland when she was only six days old.

00:30:09.134 --> 00:30:34.666
Chapter 6 Highlands and Islands The Highlands in the north and west of Scotland are a special part of the world.

00:30:34.726 --> 00:30:46.879
There are not a lot of people here, only eight people per square kilometre, but there is a lot of beautiful country.

00:30:46.939 --> 00:30:51.326
with locks, rivers and hills.

00:30:51.386 --> 00:31:07.261
The Highlands have two of the most wonderful train journeys in the world, from Inverness to Kyle of Lohausch and from Fort William to Mallegge.

00:31:07.321 --> 00:31:18.371
Or visitors can go by road past the beautiful Loch Lomond to the mountains at Glencoe.

00:31:18.431 --> 00:31:30.955
Do you love to be out of the cities, walking, climbing, looking for birds and animals or taking photos?

00:31:31.015 --> 00:31:34.666
Then the highlands are for you.

00:31:34.726 --> 00:31:39.797
The mountains are not very high.

00:31:39.857 --> 00:31:52.006
The highest in the UK is 1,344 metres, but they can be difficult.

00:31:52.066 --> 00:32:06.498
Sometimes cold weather comes from the north, so walkers and climbers need to be careful in the winter and in the summer too.

00:32:06.558 --> 00:32:11.302
What can you see in the Highlands?

00:32:11.362 --> 00:32:20.378
There are not many trees on the hills, but there is beautiful heaven.

00:32:20.438 --> 00:32:26.674
The water in the locks is cold and dark.

00:32:26.734 --> 00:32:37.661
Red deer run across the hills, and perhaps you can see golden eagles high up in the sky.

00:32:37.721 --> 00:32:44.378
When you leave the road, You are soon in empty country.

00:32:44.438 --> 00:32:53.942
There is nothing but the hills and the sky, the birds and the animals.

00:32:54.002 --> 00:33:05.476
Islands Scotland has hundreds of islands and life is different there.

00:33:05.536 --> 00:33:09.477
The island towns are small.

00:33:09.537 --> 00:33:20.328
On some islands, like sky and the islands of the Hebrides, many of the people speak Gaelic.

00:33:20.388 --> 00:33:33.462
On the islands of Lewis and Harris, some people do not work, drive or watch TV on Sundays.

00:33:33.522 --> 00:33:44.044
In the North, the islands of Orkney and Shetland are nearer to Norway than to London.

00:33:44.105 --> 00:33:53.738
From Obern, in the west of Scotland, you can go to some of the islands in the inner Hebrides.

00:33:53.798 --> 00:34:00.272
Here, you can see sea animals, like seals.

00:34:00.332 --> 00:34:06.924
From Mull, you can go to see the beautiful caves of Stapha.

00:34:06.984 --> 00:34:10.757
or you can visit Iona.

00:34:10.817 --> 00:34:25.120
Saint Colombo came to the small island of Iona from Ireland in 563 and began to teach people about Christianity.

00:34:25.180 --> 00:34:30.997
Today Iona is an important Christian centre.

00:34:31.057 --> 00:34:36.309
and half a million people go there every year.

00:34:36.369 --> 00:34:44.872
The bodies of Kenneth MacAlpin and other Scottish kings are here on Iona.

00:34:44.932 --> 00:34:48.340
You can also go to Skye.

00:34:48.400 --> 00:35:03.106
Perhaps the most famous and the most beautiful of the islands of Scotland and see the dark mountains called the Kulin near the sea.

00:35:03.166 --> 00:35:11.072
From other pool you can go across to Lewis in the outer hebrides.

00:35:11.132 --> 00:35:16.286
There are long white empty beaches here.

00:35:16.346 --> 00:35:22.226
and the 5,000 year old stone circle at Kalanish.

00:35:22.286 --> 00:35:27.612
There are 50 standing stones at Kalanish.

00:35:27.672 --> 00:35:41.206
Time goes more slowly in this quiet special place.

00:35:41.266 --> 00:36:10.219
Chapter 7 Sport and Free Time Many Scottish people love sport, and in Scotland many people play golf, the rich and the not so rich.

00:36:10.279 --> 00:36:14.576
Golf began in this country.

00:36:14.636 --> 00:36:25.317
and the golf capital of Scotland is St Andrews, a small university city near Edinburgh.

00:36:25.378 --> 00:36:35.654
There are more than 400 golf courses in Scotland and many of them are very beautiful.

00:36:35.714 --> 00:36:47.509
In the summer when the days are long you can play from seven in the morning to ten at night.

00:36:47.569 --> 00:36:52.732
Football is also very important.

00:36:52.792 --> 00:37:04.390
In 2006, Scotland had the tenth highest number of football clubs of any country in the world.

00:37:04.450 --> 00:37:20.908
Celtic and Rangers are the biggest and the most famous Scottish clubs but others like Aberdeen also do well in Scotland and in Europe.

00:37:20.968 --> 00:37:29.904
The Scots play rugby football too and it is an important sport in Scotland.

00:37:29.964 --> 00:37:47.092
In 1990 Scotland beat England, Wales Ireland and France at Rugby, and every year the Scots want to do this again.

00:37:47.152 --> 00:37:57.859
But now Italy plays too, so they need to beat five countries, not four.

00:37:57.919 --> 00:38:04.920
Scotland's hills and mountains are good for sports too.

00:38:04.980 --> 00:38:10.478
walking, climbing, cycling and more.

00:38:10.538 --> 00:38:24.142
And there are lots of exciting sports on, in and under the water in the locks, rivers and the sea.

00:38:24.203 --> 00:38:33.160
You can take a kayak down a fast river, for example, or out to sea.

00:38:33.221 --> 00:38:38.990
The rain does not matter when you do these sports.

00:38:39.051 --> 00:38:56.786
Between May and September, in more than a hundred different places in Scotland, people meet at Highland Games, a festival of Scottish sports and music.

00:38:56.846 --> 00:39:10.220
You can see big men tossing the camber, listen to Scottish music on the backpipes and watch Scottish dancing.

00:39:10.280 --> 00:39:24.860
Not all Scotsmen have kilts, but more and more of them wear one sometimes and lots of people wear kilts to the games.

00:39:24.920 --> 00:39:33.274
When a Scotsman wears a kilt, he is saying I like being Scottish.

00:39:33.334 --> 00:39:39.908
A kilt needs about six metres of tartan.

00:39:39.968 --> 00:39:54.306
When one of the big clans like Clann Donald, Mackenzie or Stuart has a big meeting, people come from many countries.

00:39:54.366 --> 00:40:00.353
Many of them wear kilts in the Clann tartan when they come.

00:40:00.413 --> 00:40:08.460
One clan in the USA, Clan Donald, has 4,000 families.

00:40:08.520 --> 00:40:28.385
Their families went from Scotland to the USA, perhaps 150 years ago, but they still want to be Scottish.

00:40:28.445 --> 00:40:38.794
Chapter 8.

00:40:38.814 --> 00:40:56.168
5 Great Scots Scotland is not a big country and does not have many people, but there are many famous and important Scots.

00:40:56.228 --> 00:40:59.658
Here are 5 of them.

00:40:59.718 --> 00:41:16.488
Robert Adams 1728 to 1792 made some of the best buildings in Great Britain in the second half of the 1700s.

00:41:16.508 --> 00:41:27.898
He went to school and university in Edinburgh and then to Rome for five years to learn about the buildings there.

00:41:27.958 --> 00:41:32.220
Then he came back to Britain.

00:41:32.281 --> 00:41:42.895
He worked on the new town in Edinburgh and you can see buildings by him in Charlotte Square.

00:41:42.955 --> 00:41:50.368
There are many beautiful buildings by Adam in England and Scotland.

00:41:50.428 --> 00:41:58.701
People come from all over Europe and America to look at them.

00:41:58.761 --> 00:42:10.583
David Hume 1711 to 1776 was a great thinker and writer.

00:42:10.644 --> 00:42:19.250
People still read and talk about his books today because they are so important.

00:42:19.310 --> 00:42:30.712
He also wrote a very long history of England at that time the most important book of this kind.

00:42:30.772 --> 00:42:38.756
Hume went to the University of Edinburgh when he was 12 years old.

00:42:38.816 --> 00:42:50.542
Later he went to France and made friends with famous French thinkers like Voltaire and Rousseau.

00:42:50.602 --> 00:42:57.908
His most important book is A Treatise of Human Nature.

00:42:57.968 --> 00:43:02.313
1739 to 1740.

00:43:02.373 --> 00:43:13.335
The next great Scott is Robert Burns, 1759 to 1796.

00:43:13.395 --> 00:43:20.687
The Scots call him Robbie or Robbie Burns.

00:43:20.747 --> 00:43:25.942
He was born into a poor country family.

00:43:26.002 --> 00:43:33.602
the oldest of seven children near air in southwest Scotland.

00:43:33.662 --> 00:43:39.636
He began writing poems when he was still a boy.

00:43:39.696 --> 00:43:52.530
He wrote about important things about life and love, rich people and poor people, and Scotland.

00:43:52.591 --> 00:44:01.697
His words still speak to us today, and many Scots love his poems.

00:44:01.758 --> 00:44:13.732
Burns was born on the 25th of January, and that night is called Burns' Night by the Scots.

00:44:13.792 --> 00:44:20.716
On Burns' Night there are special dinners, not just in Scotland.

00:44:20.777 --> 00:44:25.410
but for Scottish people in other countries too.

00:44:25.470 --> 00:44:36.576
They eat haggis, a special Scottish food, drink whiskey and say poems by burns.

00:44:36.636 --> 00:44:53.091
And at midnight on the 31st of December in many English speaking countries, People sing the words of birds when they sing Old Lang Zine.

00:44:53.151 --> 00:45:02.176
The name of the song means something like long, long ago.

00:45:02.236 --> 00:45:10.493
James Watt, 1736 to 1819 was born in Greenock, near Glasgow.

00:45:10.553 --> 00:45:17.750
and did not often go to school when he was a child.

00:45:17.810 --> 00:45:23.874
He stayed at home and his mother was his teacher.

00:45:23.934 --> 00:45:33.159
What was a quick thinker and he liked to build things with his hands.

00:45:33.220 --> 00:45:40.714
He got work building things for the teachers at the University of Glasgow.

00:45:40.774 --> 00:45:46.661
There, he became interested in steam engines.

00:45:46.721 --> 00:46:01.212
He began a business with his friend Matthew Bolton and from 1794 to 1824 they made 1,164 steam engines.

00:46:01.272 --> 00:46:13.402
These engines changed Great Britain and the world.

00:46:13.462 --> 00:46:15.212
After what?

00:46:15.272 --> 00:46:20.143
The world was a different place.

00:46:20.204 --> 00:46:37.306
Sir Arthur Conan Doyle, 1859 to 1930, was born in Edinburgh, went to the University of Edinburgh and became a doctor.

00:46:37.366 --> 00:46:42.895
but he did not make much money as a doctor.

00:46:42.955 --> 00:46:52.380
So he began to write stories about a detective called Sherlock Holmes.

00:46:52.440 --> 00:47:02.259
Soon Sherlock Holmes was famous and Conan Doyle became rich and famous too.

00:47:02.320 --> 00:47:17.792
He wrote many stories about Holmes and his friend Dr Watson and also wrote stories about a man called Professor Challenger.

00:47:17.852 --> 00:47:28.054
There are films of many Sherlock Holmes stories, and you can read some of them in bookworms.

00:47:28.114 --> 00:47:36.576
Sherlock Holmes and the Duke's son, and Sherlock Holmes and the Sport of Kings.

00:47:36.636 --> 00:47:40.487
Oxford bookworms, stage one.

00:47:40.547 --> 00:47:46.270
Do you know any other great Scots?

00:47:46.330 --> 00:48:08.130
In the earliest days, Scottish people took their food from the sea, the rivers and the land.

00:48:08.190 --> 00:48:37.766
From the sea and the rivers came fish and from the land they got fruit, vegetables and meat and they got oats from the land too.

00:48:37.826 --> 00:48:47.736
They made their bread from oats and they made porridge from oats and water.

00:48:47.796 --> 00:48:50.480
for a hot breakfast.

00:48:50.540 --> 00:49:01.158
People still eat all of these things in Scotland today, but they eat new things too.

00:49:01.218 --> 00:49:18.322
From 1900 on, many people began to come to Scotland from countries like Italy and India, and they brought different food with them.

00:49:18.382 --> 00:49:24.028
There is a lot of good food in Scotland.

00:49:24.089 --> 00:49:34.082
It is famous for its fish like salmon and paddock and for other food from the sea.

00:49:34.142 --> 00:49:39.994
Fish farming is very important for Scotland.

00:49:40.054 --> 00:49:44.612
It gives work to about 6,000 people.

00:49:44.672 --> 00:49:49.542
and brings lots of money into the country.

00:49:49.602 --> 00:50:05.250
Then there is Scottish beef, the best beef in the world the Scots say, and haggis made from meat, oats and other things.

00:50:05.310 --> 00:50:10.564
Scottish raspberries are also very good.

00:50:10.624 --> 00:50:20.800
The Scots like to make a dish called kranakim with raspberries, oats and whiskey.

00:50:20.860 --> 00:50:28.674
People with lots of money can go to a hotel like Glen Eagles near Perth.

00:50:28.734 --> 00:50:34.006
perhaps the most famous hotel in Scotland today.

00:50:34.066 --> 00:50:45.561
It has 232 bedrooms, three golf courses and some of the best Scottish food.

00:50:45.621 --> 00:50:58.936
But good food is not a part of life for everybody in Scotland and some poorer Scots do not eat very well.

00:50:58.996 --> 00:51:13.734
British men usually live to 77 years old, but in some parts of Glasgow they only live to 54.

00:51:13.794 --> 00:51:24.354
Better food is very important for Scottish people today and tomorrow.

00:51:24.414 --> 00:51:33.865
The word whiskey comes from the Gaelic, whiskey by the water of life.

00:51:33.925 --> 00:51:41.010
Whiskey, also called Scotch, is made in a distillery.

00:51:41.071 --> 00:51:59.670
There are more than 100 distilleries in Scotland, some near Edinburgh and Glasgow in the south, one on the island of Skye, and three on the Orkneys in the North.

00:51:59.730 --> 00:52:05.163
There are nine on the small island of Eila.

00:52:05.224 --> 00:52:12.408
About half of the distilleries are near the river Spay, east of Inverness.

00:52:12.468 --> 00:52:20.692
Many visitors like to visit the distilleries there and try the different whiskeys.

00:52:20.752 --> 00:52:30.188
Visitors to Edinburgh can visit the Scotch Whiskey Heritage Centre near the castle.

00:52:30.248 --> 00:52:37.311
Whiskey is good, but another drink in Scotland is better.

00:52:37.371 --> 00:52:39.092
Water.

00:52:39.152 --> 00:52:46.846
The whiskey is good because the water from the hills is good.

00:52:46.906 --> 00:52:58.234
On a hot day, a drink of Scottish hill water can be the best drink in the world.

00:52:58.294 --> 00:53:23.168
Chapter 10 Scotland and the World Scotland is a small country.

00:53:23.228 --> 00:53:28.100
Only five million people live there.

00:53:28.160 --> 00:53:37.556
But for millions more across the world, Scotland is very important.

00:53:37.616 --> 00:53:40.816
Why is this?

00:53:40.876 --> 00:53:50.970
In the 1800s, many people left Scotland and went to other countries.

00:53:51.030 --> 00:54:03.174
People in the Highlands left their homes and villages because they were very poor and hungry.

00:54:03.234 --> 00:54:10.086
Sometimes the rich people there wanted them to leave.

00:54:10.146 --> 00:54:23.126
Many others from the south of Scotland left because they wanted a better life in a new country.

00:54:23.186 --> 00:54:44.538
Between the 1820s and 1914, more than 2 million people went from Scotland across the seas to the United States, Canada, Australia and New Zealand.

00:54:44.598 --> 00:54:48.710
Moore went in the 1920s.

00:54:48.770 --> 00:54:56.704
Today, there are six million Scottish Americans in the USA.

00:54:56.764 --> 00:55:05.890
Every year, many people walk through New York on the 6th of April, tartan day.

00:55:05.950 --> 00:55:13.040
A lot of Scottish Americans go back to Scotland as tourists.

00:55:13.100 --> 00:55:20.148
they want to find their past and to understand it.

00:55:20.208 --> 00:55:28.562
So Scotland is important in the story of other countries too.

00:55:28.622 --> 00:55:45.091
Two great Scots in the USA are Alexander Graham Bell 1847-1922 and Andrew Carnegie 1837 to 1919.

00:55:45.151 --> 00:55:47.082
Bell made the first telephone.

00:55:47.102 --> 00:55:56.229
He began the Bell Telephone Company in 1877 and by 1885, more than 150,000 people in the USA had telephones.

00:55:56.269 --> 00:56:24.497
In 1915, He made the first telephone call across the United States from New York to San Francisco.

00:56:24.557 --> 00:56:39.314
After he died in Canada at the age of 75, all the telephones in North America were quiet for one minute to remember him.

00:56:39.374 --> 00:56:49.030
Andrew Carnegie's family left Scotland when he was 11 and went to the USA.

00:56:49.091 --> 00:57:04.286
Carnegie worked hard and by the 1880s he had many businesses and was very rich, the richest man in the world.

00:57:04.346 --> 00:57:11.506
When he stopped working he gave his money to other people.

00:57:11.566 --> 00:57:27.632
Carnegie's money built schools, universities and other buildings in the USA, the UK, Australia, New Zealand and Ireland.

00:57:27.692 --> 00:57:37.947
Today, his money still helps millions of people around the world every year.

00:57:38.007 --> 00:57:45.982
Many Scots love to visit other countries and do new things.

00:57:46.042 --> 00:58:02.370
David Livingston, 1813 to 1873, went to Africa to begin schools and to tell Africans about Christianity.

00:58:02.430 --> 00:58:25.382
He was the first European to see the Victoria Falls between Zambia and Zimbabwe and the first to go across Africa from the Atlantic in the West to the Indian Ocean in the East.

00:58:25.442 --> 00:58:31.548
Alan Bean, born 1932, is a Scottish American.

00:58:31.608 --> 00:58:43.328
When he went to the moon in 1969, only the fourth man to walk on the moon.

00:58:43.388 --> 00:58:47.090
He took some tartan with him.

00:58:47.150 --> 00:58:52.813
Scots like to go to new places.

00:58:52.873 --> 00:58:59.766
Who is the most famous Scot in the world today?

00:58:59.826 --> 00:59:02.010
To many people.

00:59:02.071 --> 00:59:08.904
It is the film star, Sean Connery, born 1930.

00:59:08.964 --> 00:59:19.256
Sean Connery did a lot of different jobs before he began working in television and cinema.

00:59:19.316 --> 00:59:25.818
Then, in 1962, he was James Bond.

00:59:25.878 --> 00:59:36.342
In the first James Bond film, Doctor No, After this, he was famous everywhere.

00:59:36.402 --> 00:59:45.728
He made six more James Bond films and made many other films after that.

00:59:45.788 --> 00:59:54.792
To many people, his best film is The Untouchables, 1987.

00:59:54.852 --> 01:00:20.286
Connery does not make films now and does not live in Scotland, but he loves Scotland very much and does not want it to be part of the United Kingdom.

01:00:20.346 --> 01:00:48.817
Chapter 11 Scotland Today and Tomorrow The story of Scotland is interesting and sometimes exciting, but it is not an easy story.

01:00:48.877 --> 01:01:02.550
Some Scots, like Sean Connery, want Scotland to be just Scotland and not part of the United Kingdom.

01:01:02.610 --> 01:01:09.561
Scotland is still a country of rich and poor.

01:01:09.621 --> 01:01:22.744
The past is important, but many Scots also want to think about Scotland today and tomorrow.

01:01:22.804 --> 01:01:39.172
The Scots in the USA, Australia, Canada and New Zealand love the old things, the music, the dancing, the tartans.

01:01:39.232 --> 01:01:51.252
But many of the Scots in Scotland want their country to look to the future, not the past.

01:01:51.312 --> 01:02:06.180
Scotland, they say, can be like Norway, a country with 5 million people and with a lot of money from oil.

01:02:06.240 --> 01:02:20.815
Scotland has money from oil, fish farming, visits from tourists, banks, computers and many other businesses.

01:02:20.876 --> 01:02:30.724
And there is new life here, new business, new cinema, new music.

01:02:30.784 --> 01:02:35.306
Things are changing in Scotland.

01:02:35.366 --> 01:02:41.770
Today there is a Scottish Parliament in Edinburgh.

01:02:41.830 --> 01:02:47.314
It is in an interesting new building on the Royal Mile.

01:02:47.374 --> 01:02:57.554
People from every town and city in Scotland come here to talk about their country.

01:02:57.614 --> 01:03:07.572
Some Scots want Scotland to speak for itself, in Europe and in the world.

01:03:07.632 --> 01:03:12.599
What is in the future for Scotland?

01:03:12.659 --> 01:03:23.032
Nobody knows, but it is always going to be a beautiful, special place.

01:03:23.092 --> 01:03:39.476
Perhaps one day you can walk along the Royal Mile, climb the hills or travel to the islands and see it for yourself.

01:03:39.536 --> 01:03:42.698
Oh.

